.TH mbsvpextract 1 "4 November 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbsvpextract\fP \- Extract World Ocean Atlas sound velocity profiles at the
sites of a site file or the waypoints of a route file.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbsvpextract\fP
.br
--input=file {-Ifile} (or --sites=file or --route=file)
.br
--svpfolder=directory {-Sdirectory}
.br
--outputfolder=directory {-Odirectory}
.br
--woa-version=18|23
.br
--period=period {-Pperiod}
.br
--resolution=01|04 {-R01|04}
.br
--formula=chen|leroy|both
.br
--strict-nearest
.br
--overwrite
.br
--help {-H}
.br
--verbose {-V}

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbsvpextract\fP writes annual sound velocity profiles (SVPs) from a World Ocean
Atlas sound speed grid for positions chosen by the user. It does not need swath
data, so it can prepare profiles for earlier cruises and for planned surveys. A profile
is a climatological candidate, not a forecast or a measurement of the water
column at that position. \fBmbsvpbest\fP(1) can compare candidates on swath data.

.B Positions
.br
The positions come from an MB-System site file (.ste), with one profile for each site, or
from a route file (.rte), with one profile for each waypoint. These files are
described in \fBmbgrdviz\fP(1). A waypoint is a numeric line with a comment starting with
"## WAYPOINT", such as "## WAYPOINT" or "## WAYPOINT STARTLINE". Numeric lines without
this comment only give the topography between waypoints and are not used, and a
file with no such lines is an error. Only the position is read, and the topography
in the file plays no part. A file with several routes gives waypoints for each of them.

.B Sound speed grids
.br
The program requires at least one of the sound speed datasets posted at
https://zenodo.org/communities/mb-system_woa_svp_datasets/
.br
You name the grid to use with \fB\-\-woa-version\fP, \fB\-\-period\fP and
\fB\-\-resolution\fP; none of them has a default, because the datasets installed differ
from one user to another. The grid is the annual file
woaYY_PERIOD_svp00_RR.nc in the directory given with \fB\-\-svpfolder\fP. It must contain
the variable v_an_chen, v_an_leroy or both, as needed for \fB\-\-formula\fP, on the
dimensions depth, lat and lon.

.B Grid cell
.br
Each position takes the profile of the nearest grid cell that can be completed
to 11000 m, among the 16 nearest cells, or only the nearest with
\fB\-\-strict-nearest\fP. Using another cell than the nearest gives a warning. All wanted
formulas must complete in the same cell. A position outside a cropped grid fails. One cell can serve
several positions; each still gets its own file.

.B Completion to 11000 m
.br
Invalid samples are removed, and a profile must start within 1 m of the surface. A profile
that stops short continues through nearby deeper cells of the same grid, stepping to
deeper ones until none lies within 8 degrees. The shape of the neighbour is used, with an offset
at the join that fades with depth so that nothing jumps. A neighbour that differs by more than 10 m/s
at the join is refused, because it likely holds another water mass, such as across a sill. Below that,
only pressure changes the speed: potential temperature and salinity are held at their values in the
last sample, assuming a salinity of 35 to find the temperature. Full depth CTD casts from hadal
trenches support this (Kolbusz and Jamieson, 2024). A tail is a processing estimate, not an observation.

.B Files
.br
Profiles are named after the site, or after the route and waypoint number counted from zero
such as Route_1_WP000.svp, using safe characters only. With \fB\-\-formula=both\fP
the names end in _chen and _leroy. The first comment gives the formula, period and
position name, and a comment starting with # MBSVPEXTRACT_LOCATION gives the requested position,
the position of the grid cell, whether a deeper neighbour was used and the depth where the pressure-only
tail starts. A position that fails is recorded and the next one
goes on. Each run also writes a new summary, mbsvpextract-DATE-TIME.json, updated after each
position.

